-- title:
--   Supersingular inertia eigenvector via level-two fundamental characters, weight two
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $\mathbb{Z}$ with $\Delta(W)\neq 0$ which is a semistable model, i.e. $q\mid\Delta(W)$ implies $q\nmid c_4(W)$ for every prime $q$, and whose $p$-torsion representation is irreducible: the $p$-torsion of $W$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is nontrivial and its only Galois-stable $\mathbb{Z}/p$-submodules are $0$ and the whole. Let $N'\geq 1$ with $p\nmid N'$, let $S_0$ be a finite set of naturals with $p\in S_0$, let $k'=2$ as an integer, let $i\in\mathbb{N}$, and let $\mathfrak{m}'$ be a maximal ideal of the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N' k' S₀`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight-$k'$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N')$ generated by the $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid N'$, $\ell\notin S_0$, and the $U_q$ for primes $q\mid N'$, $q\notin S_0$) with $p\in\mathfrak{m}'$ and $T_\ell-\ell^{\,i}a_\ell(W)\in\mathfrak{m}'$ for every prime $\ell\notin S_0$, $\ell\nmid N'$ with $\ell\nmid\Delta(W)$, where $a_\ell(W)=\ell+1-\#W_{\mathbb{F}_\ell}$. Let $F$ be a field of characteristic $p$ and $\theta$ a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N' k' (S₀ \ {p})`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) to $F$ whose restriction along the inclusion of the algebra for $S_0$ has kernel $\mathfrak{m}'$, with $\theta(T_p)=0$. Let $k_n\in\mathbb{N}$ with $k_n=k'$, and let $\rho\colon\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})\to\mathrm{GL}_2(F)$ be a homomorphism trivial on the subgroup fixing some finite subextension of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$, such that for every prime $\ell\notin S_0$, $\ell\nmid N'$, $\ell\nmid\Delta(W)$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $A$ and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition group of $A$ acting as $x\mapsto x^\ell$ on the residue field of $A$, one has $\mathrm{tr}\,\rho(\sigma)=\theta(T_\ell)$ and $\det\rho(\sigma)=\ell^{\,k_n-1}$. Finally let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $P$, let $\pi\in\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfy $\pi^{p^2-1}=p$, and let $\psi\colon F\to\kappa(P)$ be a ring homomorphism. Then there is a nonzero $v\in\kappa(P)^2$ such that, writing $\omega(\sigma)$ for the tame character $\sigma\mapsto$ residue of $\sigma\pi/\pi$ (and $0$ if $\sigma\pi/\pi\notin P$), either $(\psi\rho(\sigma))v=\omega(\sigma)^{k_n-1}v$ for all $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, or $(\psi\rho(\sigma))v=(\omega(\sigma)^p)^{k_n-1}v$ for all such $\sigma$.
--
--   This is the representation-side form, with the determinant prescribed, of the supersingular case of Fontaine's and Edixhoven's analysis of the weight in Serre's conjecture: when the $T_p$-eigenvalue vanishes, inertia at $p$ has an eigenvector on which it acts through a power of one of the two fundamental characters of level two. It is the specialisation of the general statement, which ranges over the weight window $2\le k'\le p+1$, to the single weight $k'=2$, and it feeds the corresponding statement at weight $k'=3$.
